// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: types, generics, arity, kinds
// @expected-error: E407
//
// UNMUTED 2026-09-07. The skip directive here named T0922, which is
// CLOSED. Re-measured with the directive removed: this file
// raises `error<E407>`, its exact `@expected-error`.
//
// A guard muted on a reason that has expired is a guard that
// cannot report a regression, and the suite counts it as
// skipped rather than as missing — the quietest way to lose
// coverage.
// @description: Writing brackets applies a type constructor, so the
//   number of arguments must match the declaration — in BOTH directions.
//
// The arity check existed and asked only `provided > expected`:
//
//     let p: Pair<Int, Text, Bool> = …;   // refused, 3 > 2
//     let p: Pair<Int>             = …;   // ACCEPTED
//
// The half that was missing is the worse half. Too many arguments is a
// loud mistake with nowhere to go. Too FEW leaves a parameter free, and
// inference then unifies it with whatever arrives first — so the program
// does not fail, it quietly means something else, and the wrong answer
// surfaces far from the annotation that caused it.
//
// UNDER-APPLICATION IS NOT ITSELF THE ERROR. A bare `List` is the
// unapplied constructor, and a higher-kinded position wants exactly
// that. The rule keys on the BRACKETS: write them and you have applied
// the constructor, so the count has to match; write the bare name and
// you have named it, so there is nothing to count. Its positive pole
// (`generic_arity_the_bare_name_is_not_an_application.vr`) is what keeps
// this file from being read as "saturate everything".

public type Pair<A, B> is { a: A, b: B };

// ERROR: `Pair` takes two type arguments, one was supplied.
public fn take(p: Pair<Int>) -> Int {
    p.a
}

fn main() {
    print(f"{take(Pair { a: 1, b: 2 })}");
}
